    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Nitrification, the oxidation of ammonium (NH4+) to nitrate (NO3-) by ammonia-oxidizing bacteria (AOB) and archaea (AOA), is a crucial step in removing nitrogen from marine ecosystems. NO3- is converted to N2 gas in a subsequent transformation and exit, freeing the ecosystem of a nutrient that in excess can lead to excessive productivity, hypoxia, and other downstream consequences. The Sacramento/San Joaquin River delta receives NH4+-laden effluent from the Sacramento Regional Wastewater Treatment Plant (SRWTP). The system provides freshwater to irrigate the Central Valley and drinking water for many millions of people. Studying the rate of nitrification and the composition of the microbial community will further elucidate how nutrient loading has impacted this ecosystem. Nitrification rates were calculated from water samples collected in the Sacramento River starting upstream of the SRWTP then moving downstream below its outfall. DNA and RNA were extracted from filtered water at each site and qPCR assays were used targeting the amoA gene, which encodes the α-subunit of ammonia monooxygenase, the enzyme catalyzing the oxidation of ammonium to nitrite (NO2-).  AOA transcripts predominated among AOA and AOB. Nitrifying microbes provide an ecological service in reducing ammonium concentration, a goal of wastewater regulation. Future monitoring of the activity of the Sacramento River’s nitrifying microorganisms will be important for analyzing how this ecosystem responds to future changes that will impact both people and wildlife.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
